      "live": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-332541",
        "problem": "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=12x+8.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
        "fields": [
          {
            "key": "field-1",
            "type": "blank",
            "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
            "tag": "mathquill",
            "choices": [],
            "dom_index": 0,
            "disabled": false,
            "input_html": "<div id=\"freeResponseTextbox-1\" class=\"matheditor-wrapper-answer\" ;=\"\" style=\"min-width:110px;\n                                        margin-left:0px;\n                                        margin-right:0px;\n                                        background:;\"><div class=\"mq-editable-field mq-math-mode\" style=\"border: none; outline: none;\"><span class=\"mq-textarea\"><textarea autocapitalize=\"off\" autocomplete=\"off\" autocorrect=\"off\" spellcheck=\"false\" x-palm-disable-ste-all=\"true\" style=\"\" disabled=\"\"></textarea></span><span class=\"mq-root-block\" mathquill-block-id=\"19\"><var mathquill-command-id=\"21\">A</var><var mathquill-command-id=\"23\">e</var><span class=\"mq-supsub mq-non-leaf mq-sup-only\" mathquill-command-id=\"25\"><span class=\"mq-sup\" mathquill-block-id=\"27\"><span mathquill-command-id=\"26\" class=\"\">\u2212</span><span mathquill-command-id=\"28\">6</span><var mathquill-command-id=\"30\">x</var></span></span><span mathquill-command-id=\"33\" class=\"mq-binary-operator\">+</span><span mathquill-command-id=\"35\">2</span><var mathquill-command-id=\"37\">x</var><span mathquill-command-id=\"39\" class=\"mq-binary-operator\">+</span><span mathquill-command-id=\"41\">1</span></span></div></div>",
            "rendered_text": "Ae\u22126x+2x+1",
            "choices_complete": false
          }
        ]
      },
      "history": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-332541",
        "sequence_position": 3,
        "grade": "Correct",
        "worked_solution": "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\u2032}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=12x+8\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(\\alpha +6\\beta )=12x+8\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +6\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x}+2x+1.\n$$",
